Consider the following rule,

 rule **/*.o -> exe

If the LHS matches anything, exe is the target of some dependency and can become invalidated.

But if the LHS has no matches, then no dependency exists with exe in its targets set, and hence
exe cannot become invalidated (it is then considered a so-called primary file).

There is some loss of information here.

In this case, there should be a dependency hyperedge with zero incident vertices in the downward
direction. Normally the generate-pattern is not executed at all, when the match pattern has no
results.

On the other hand, consider the following rule,

 rule **/*.c -> $^D/$.o

Here, if the LHS has no matches, then no dependency edges should be created.

The lack of any reference to match context needs to be noticed when the generate pattern is parsed.
